{"state": {"case_id": "MB_18", "timestep": 2, "case_summary": "A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain. The pain has been dull and progressive, but became suddenly worse while she was exercising. Her medical history is notable for depression, anxiety, and gonococcal urethritis that was appropriately treated. She is sexually active and does not use condoms. She admits to drinking at least 5 standard alcoholic drinks a day. The patient also recently lost a large amount of weight for a fitness show she planned on entering. The patient's current medications include oral contraceptive pills, fluoxetine, alprazolam, ibuprofen, acetaminophen, and folate. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 80/40 mmHg, pulse is 110/minute, and respirations are 15/minute with an oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. On physical exam, you note an athletic young woman with burly shoulders, a thick neck, and acne on her forehead and back. On abdominal exam you note diffuse tenderness with 10/10 pain upon palpation of the right upper quadrant. Intravenous fluids are started and labs are sent. A urinary ß-hCG has been ordered. Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst\nB. Inflammation of the pancreas\nC. Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein\nD. Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones\nE. Vascular ectasia within the liver\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Right Upper Quadrant Abdominal Pain with Shock of Suspected Ectopic or Hemorrhagic Aetiology", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.7, "supporting_facts": ["Severe right upper quadrant pain with diffuse tenderness", "Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) indicating shock", "High-risk sexual behavior (no condoms) and pending β-hCG test", "History of gonococcal urethritis (increased risk for tubal pathology)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute cholecystitis: less likely due to shock context and high-risk pregnancy profile", "Ruptured ovarian cyst: possible but β-hCG pending clarifies pregnancy status", "Alcohol-related liver injury: inconsistent with acute shock presentation"], "alarm_features": ["Hypovolemic shock", "Unprotected sexual activity", "Reproductive-age female with acute RUQ pain"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Pregnancy-related Hemodynamic Compromise", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.47368421052631576, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Shock during exercise is less typical for pregnancy-related compromise (which usually presents in the supine position) but the high-risk pregnancy profile main…"], "evidence_against": ["Lack of pregnancy symptoms like amenorrhea moderately reduces likelihood of pregnancy-related compromise, but β-hCG test pending maintains diagnostic considera…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Acute Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.188, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of GI bleeding or trauma weakens the case for intra-abdominal hemorrhage relative to pregnancy-related compromise, creating a moderate counterpoint."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Hepatic Vascular Occlusion", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.203, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No neurological signs (e.g., abducens palsy) reduce the likelihood of hepatic vascular occlusion, but the LR+ for weight loss/alcohol use still provides weak s…"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Bil